    <h2>What this page could not verify<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <div class="warn">
      Three things, stated plainly so you know which parts of this page are reads and which are reconstruction.
      <br><br>
      <b>1. The last digit of the passcode.</b> The graph paper's subtracted row reads <b>7 9 3 5</b> — two independent high-zoom passes agree, and the arithmetic only balances with that row (41268 − 7935 = 33333). But the four circles on the wall panel read <b>7 9 3 6</b> across five consecutive frames, and the final glyph was matched against the keypad's own 5 and 6 buttons, where it matches the <b>6</b>. Both readings are multi-pass, both are internally consistent, and they differ by exactly one stroke. Start with the paper's <b>7935</b>; if the panel refuses it, the recorded entry was <b>7936</b>. This page does not have a frame that shows the panel accepting a code, only one that shows it closing.
      <br><br>
      <b>2. What the first plate's code is.</b> The plate's track and keypad are both fully visible, but the digits only exist as the knob's position and the lit keys at a given instant, and our reads of the lit state are not stable enough to print a sequence. The method on this page — connect the contacts with the knob, then key in the matching digits, and the indicator run at the bottom tracks your progress — is what the recording shows. The sequence itself is not printed.
      <br><br>
      <b>3. What the H / N / Mg notes and the periodic table feed.</b> Both are real, readable clues sitting on the same wall, and no frame in this run shows either being used on a lock. They may belong to a step this recording skips, or to a later room. The page lists them and says nothing about a target.
    </div>
